1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,840
Okay.

2
00:00:00,860 --> 00:00:02,360
You're sitting at a desk in Mission Control,

3
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,320
and in front of you is a very big, very

4
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:05,140
red button.

5
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:06,140
No pressure.

6
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,500
Behind that button, a tower, a rocket, and

7
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:10,180
about nine tenths of it is just fuel.

8
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:11,500
Ninety percent.

9
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:14,100
The rocket is basically a giant flying petrol

10
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:16,180
tank with a tiny, very brave, passenger

11
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,000
seat on top.

12
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:18,240
Five phases.

13
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:20,940
Countdown, liftoff, staging, orbit, and

14
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:21,760
return.

15
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:22,980
But first, a quick guess.

16
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,360
How much of this thing is actually fuel?

17
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:26,480
A, a quarter.

18
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:27,380
B, half.

19
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:28,720
Or C, nearly all of it.

20
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:29,920
Lock your answer.

21
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:30,780
It's C.

22
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:31,720
About ninety percent.

23
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:33,720
Everything else, the engines, the tanks,

24
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:35,780
the capsule, the astronauts, is the leftover.

25
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:37,500
Now, ten, nine, eight.

26
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:39,660
Before the engines start, a flood of water

27
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:41,560
gets dumped on the pad, so the rocket's

28
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:43,060
own noise doesn't shake it to bits.

29
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:44,840
Seven, six, five.

30
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:46,120
Ignition.

31
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:47,660
And somewhere in the back row of mission

32
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,220
control, a very tired flight director takes

33
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:51,720
a very long sip of chai.

34
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:53,280
Next up, liftoff.

35
00:00:55,367 --> 00:00:56,167
Phase two.

36
00:00:56,367 --> 00:00:57,167
Liftoff.

37
00:00:57,247 --> 00:00:59,027
The engines roar, but the rocket doesn't

38
00:00:59,047 --> 00:00:59,847
move.

39
00:01:00,107 --> 00:01:00,387
Why?

40
00:01:00,407 --> 00:01:02,267
Because thrust has to beat weight first.

41
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:04,367
When it finally does, up she goes.

42
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:06,587
And no, the rocket does not push against

43
00:01:06,607 --> 00:01:07,407
the ground.

44
00:01:07,607 --> 00:01:09,567
It throws burning gas downwards, and the

45
00:01:09,587 --> 00:01:11,087
gas pushes the rocket back.

46
00:01:11,607 --> 00:01:13,347
Same trick as letting go of a balloon.

47
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:16,009
Except this balloon weighs five hundred

48
00:01:16,029 --> 00:01:16,567
tonnes.

49
00:01:16,587 --> 00:01:18,127
Then the rocket does something odd.

50
00:01:18,527 --> 00:01:19,627
It starts to lean over.

51
00:01:20,107 --> 00:01:20,527
Gently.

52
00:01:20,547 --> 00:01:22,327
Because getting to space is the easy part.

53
00:01:22,807 --> 00:01:24,367
Staying there needs sideways speed.

54
00:01:24,887 --> 00:01:26,367
About a minute in comes Max Q.

55
00:01:26,867 --> 00:01:28,747
The moment the air pushes back hardest.

56
00:01:29,207 --> 00:01:31,087
So the engines ease off for a moment, to

57
00:01:31,107 --> 00:01:31,907
get through it.

58
00:01:31,967 --> 00:01:32,847
Then back on full.

59
00:01:33,587 --> 00:01:35,387
Meanwhile, the astronauts feel their weight

60
00:01:35,407 --> 00:01:36,207
go up.

61
00:01:36,307 --> 00:01:37,187
Three times heavier.

62
00:01:37,787 --> 00:01:38,667
Quick, how does it feel?

63
00:01:39,067 --> 00:01:40,647
Like three of your cousins sitting on your

64
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:41,467
chest.

65
00:01:41,567 --> 00:01:42,367
Next up, staging.

66
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:45,233
Phase three.

67
00:01:45,673 --> 00:01:46,473
Staging.

68
00:01:46,553 --> 00:01:47,953
After about two and a half minutes, the

69
00:01:47,973 --> 00:01:49,373
first stage is nearly empty.

70
00:01:49,853 --> 00:01:52,213
And an empty fuel tank is just very expensive

71
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:53,033
dead weight.

72
00:01:53,253 --> 00:01:54,653
So, the rocket drops it.

73
00:01:55,113 --> 00:01:55,913
Bang.

74
00:01:55,993 --> 00:01:58,153
The first stage falls away, the second stage

75
00:01:58,173 --> 00:02:00,133
lights up, and the fairing opens like a

76
00:02:00,153 --> 00:02:02,093
clamshell, to let the satellite or capsule

77
00:02:02,113 --> 00:02:02,913
see the sky.

78
00:02:03,433 --> 00:02:04,233
Quick guess.

79
00:02:04,533 --> 00:02:05,933
What happens to the dropped stage?

80
00:02:06,473 --> 00:02:08,513
Does it, A, fall into the ocean and sink.

81
00:02:08,933 --> 00:02:09,633
B, burn up.

82
00:02:09,653 --> 00:02:12,113
Or C, fly back, turn around, and land on

83
00:02:12,133 --> 00:02:12,933
its tail.

84
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:14,133
Plot twist.

85
00:02:14,193 --> 00:02:15,313
Some of them, C.

86
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:17,873
Landing legs out, engines relit, and a boat

87
00:02:17,893 --> 00:02:20,073
in the ocean waiting, or a landing pad.

88
00:02:20,693 --> 00:02:22,753
Reusable rockets are why spaceflight is

89
00:02:22,773 --> 00:02:23,573
getting cheaper.

90
00:02:24,113 --> 00:02:25,493
Dropping weight is the whole trick.

91
00:02:25,953 --> 00:02:27,873
Every kilo you don't carry is a kilo you

92
00:02:27,893 --> 00:02:29,313
don't have to burn fuel to lift.

93
00:02:29,973 --> 00:02:30,773
Next up, orbit.

94
00:02:32,799 --> 00:02:33,599
Phase four.

95
00:02:33,999 --> 00:02:34,399
Orbit.

96
00:02:34,419 --> 00:02:35,539
And here's the big secret.

97
00:02:36,199 --> 00:02:37,039
Orbit isn't floating.

98
00:02:37,479 --> 00:02:38,299
Orbit is falling.

99
00:02:38,799 --> 00:02:41,119
A cannonball, fired sideways from a very

100
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:42,779
tall mountain, falls to the ground.

101
00:02:43,399 --> 00:02:44,939
Fire it faster, it lands farther.

102
00:02:45,559 --> 00:02:47,499
Fire it fast enough, and the ground curves

103
00:02:47,519 --> 00:02:49,519
away just as fast as the ball falls.

104
00:02:49,959 --> 00:02:51,659
It keeps falling, and keeps missing.

105
00:02:52,199 --> 00:02:53,845
That speed is about twenty eight thousand

106
00:02:53,865 --> 00:02:54,939
kilometres an hour.

107
00:02:55,499 --> 00:02:56,299
Quick guess.

108
00:02:56,379 --> 00:02:58,219
How long does one lap around the Earth take

109
00:02:58,239 --> 00:02:58,879
at that speed?

110
00:02:58,899 --> 00:02:59,899
Ten minutes?

111
00:02:59,919 --> 00:03:00,959
Ninety minutes?

112
00:03:01,219 --> 00:03:01,779
Or a whole day?

113
00:03:01,799 --> 00:03:02,679
Ninety minutes.

114
00:03:03,119 --> 00:03:05,219
So the crew sees about sixteen sunrises

115
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:06,219
every single day.

116
00:03:06,719 --> 00:03:08,319
And the crew floats, because they, and the

117
00:03:08,339 --> 00:03:09,979
whole station, are falling together.

118
00:03:10,659 --> 00:03:12,539
Weightless, doesn't mean gravity is gone.

119
00:03:12,959 --> 00:03:14,539
It means nothing is holding you up.

120
00:03:14,939 --> 00:03:16,739
And somewhere down there, it's your cousin's

121
00:03:16,759 --> 00:03:17,559
wedding.

122
00:03:17,619 --> 00:03:18,439
Next up, return.

123
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:21,333
Phase five.

124
00:03:21,693 --> 00:03:22,493
Return.

125
00:03:22,553 --> 00:03:24,053
Coming back is the hardest part.

126
00:03:24,573 --> 00:03:27,013
The capsule fires its engines, slows down,

127
00:03:27,193 --> 00:03:29,012
and drops into the air at twenty eight thousand

128
00:03:29,032 --> 00:03:29,993
kilometres an hour.

129
00:03:30,513 --> 00:03:32,133
And the air in front of it gets squashed

130
00:03:32,153 --> 00:03:33,413
so hard, it glows.

131
00:03:34,453 --> 00:03:34,933
Plasma.

132
00:03:34,953 --> 00:03:36,013
Around fifteen hundred degrees.

133
00:03:36,593 --> 00:03:38,233
That's not friction, that's compression.

134
00:03:38,693 --> 00:03:40,533
So the heat shield takes the heat, and gets

135
00:03:40,553 --> 00:03:42,073
sacrificed, bit by bit.

136
00:03:42,533 --> 00:03:43,773
Then comes the quiet bit.

137
00:03:44,233 --> 00:03:45,753
Small parachutes, then big ones.

138
00:03:46,353 --> 00:03:48,373
Slower, slower, and a gentle splash in the

139
00:03:48,393 --> 00:03:49,193
ocean.

140
00:03:49,293 --> 00:03:50,093
Welcome home.

141
00:03:50,233 --> 00:03:52,393
The recovery boat arrives, the door opens,

142
00:03:52,433 --> 00:03:54,193
and the first thing the astronaut asks for

143
00:03:54,213 --> 00:03:54,853
is, chai.

144
00:03:54,873 --> 00:03:55,673
Of course.

145
00:03:56,073 --> 00:03:56,813
So.

146
00:03:56,833 --> 00:03:58,973
Countdown, liftoff, staging, orbit, return.

147
00:03:59,633 --> 00:04:02,013
That's how nine tenths fuel, one tiny capsule,

148
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:04,673
and a very brave tired flight director actually

149
00:04:04,693 --> 00:04:05,493
reach space.

150
00:04:05,973 --> 00:04:06,773
And back.

151
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:07,793
Please remain seated.

152
00:04:08,273 --> 00:04:09,733
We are, technically, still falling.
